# Configuration for the integration tests.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is gitignored. Real environment variables take precedence over this
# file, so CI can inject the same names as secrets without a .env present.
#
# Run with:  npx nx integration-test @read-every-word/api-integration-test


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Storage. Required.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These tests read and write real tables and blobs. Point this at a dedicated
# dev storage account, never production.
#
# For a fully offline run, use Azurite. In a second terminal:
#
#   npx azurite --silent --location ./.azurite
#
# then create the two tables once, which Terraform normally provisions:
#
#   node -e "const {TableClient}=require('@azure/data-tables'); \
#     for (const t of ['readingCycle','readingRecord']) \
#       TableClient.fromConnectionString('UseDevelopmentStorage=true',t) \
#         .createTable().catch(()=>{})"
#
# and set the value below to exactly:
#
#   TABLE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING=UseDevelopmentStorage=true
#
# Use that shorthand rather than spelling out the endpoints. The storage SDKs
# refuse a plain http endpoint unless allowInsecureConnection is set, which the
# api does not set, but they special case the development storage shorthand.
TABLE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity. Nothing to configure.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tokens are signed by createTestIdentityProvider() from
# @read-every-word/test-utils, which generates a keypair and serves a JWKS on
# loopback. The api runs its real RS256 verification against it, so no tenant,
# no machine to machine application, and no client secret are involved.
#
# Each test also gets its own subject, which the auth middleware turns into its
# own authId and therefore its own storage partition.
